<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lynden Pindling</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1930–2000</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lynden Pindling was a Bahamian politician who led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/progressive-liberal-party">Progressive Liberal Party</a> to power in 1967, ending the rule of the white merchant elite known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-street-boys">Bay Street Boys</a>. As premier and then prime minister he led the Bahamas to independence in 1973 and governed until 1992, and he is called the father of the nation. His later years in office were marred by allegations of corruption linked to drug trafficking.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lynden-pindling.jpg" alt="Lynden Pindling" width="320" height="444" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AP Newsfeatures · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lynden_Pindling_headshot_1971.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
